 revenue-sharing financing model for AI cloud providers; the report gives no amount or date for the launch.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has launched a revenue-sharing financing model aimed at AI cloud providers, facilitating access to its computing infrastructure.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e19, e52; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e41 [fact; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Nvidia's financing model lets AI cloud operators procure its infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to cloud services sold using that capacity.\\n    Quote: Under this financing structure, AI cloud operators can procure Nvidia infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to the cloud services sold based on that capacity.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e19, e69; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e50 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e 2026-07-06] NVIDIA had launched a revenue-sharing compute program for AI startups; the item gives no launch date or program amount.\\n    Quote: The chipmaker, fresh off launching a revenue-sharing compute program for AI startups, has faced this script before.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e8; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e52 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started providing neoclouds take-or-pay minimum-revenue guarantees on GPU rental capacity in exchange for a share of revenue above the backstop level.\\n    Quote: This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e40; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e53 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started backstopping neocloud data-center leases.\\n    Quote: Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\",\n   \"- e54 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis says NVIDIA’s backstop program typically lasts six years and commits NVIDIA to purchase compute at pre-agreed prices that vary over the term.\\n    Quote: Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\",\n   \"- e55 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis says lenders underwrite NVIDIA-backstopped GPU financing to a minimum 1.3x debt-service coverage ratio, typically corresponding to a 70–80% loan-to-value ratio.\\n    Quote: When it comes to the Nvidia backstop, banks look at the DSCR ratios assuming revenue earned under the scenario where the backstop is activated. They are looking for a DSCR of at least 1.3x, particularly for the first few years of GPU financing agreement and size their loans to ensure that this bar is met. This typically corresponds to a 70-80% loan to value (LTV) ratio.\",\n   \"- e56 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis models a 25.4% project IRR for a neocloud’s one-year-or-shorter GPU rental book with NVIDIA’s backstop, potentially higher if rental rates decline less than modeled.\\n    Quote: We see that the short-term 1y rental book scenario clearly offers the best IRR among the backstopped scenarios at 25.4%, and these IRRs could realize even higher if the rental market turns out to be strong enough such that 1y and shorter rental rates decay by less than we modeled in the curve below.\",\n   \"- e57 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis models up to a 40.7% IRR for one-year GPU rentals without a backstop, versus zero or negative IRRs if a neocloud must rent to NVIDIA under the backstop.\\n    Quote: As expected, the IRRs are higher without a backstop – reaching as high as 40.7% when renting for 1y tenors with no backstop. But if Neoclouds activate the backstop and rent to Nvidia at those prices, they can expect to earn zero or negative IRRs.\",\n   \"- e58 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis models neocloud profit-before-tax margins falling from 14.8% to 5.4% when financing cost rises from 5.62% to 10%.\\n    Quote: In our modelling, we show that increasing funding from an all-in cost of 5.62% to an all-in cost of 10% drops PBT margins from 14.8% down to only 5.4%.\",\n   \"- e59 [party_statement; SemiAnalysis; original source SharonAI ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SharonAI’s June 2026 Australia AI-factory deal includes a six-year NVIDIA backstop for up to 40,000 GB300s valued at $4.88 billion, or about $2.33 per GPU-hour on average; SharonAI says its footprint is set to reach 132 MW, with 102 MW contracted, and guides to more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs by mid-2027.\\n    Quote: The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e34; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e60 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus 2026-06-29; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Firmus announced a 360 MW AI cluster project in Batam, Indonesia, on June 29, 2026, as part of the NVIDIA-backstop rollout.\\n    Quote: Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e17; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e61 [party_statement; SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Firmus expects $25–30 billion of customer revenue over six years from its project; SemiAnalysis expects Firmus to share some revenue above the NVIDIA backstop level with NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\",\n   \"- e67 [fact; futurumgroup.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA introduced a revenue-sharing and credit-support model for AI-cloud operators deploying AI factories on July 1, 2026.\\n    Quote: On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA introduced a business model that lets AI cloud operators deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories through a combination of revenue sharing and credit support, with the resulting facilities built as DSX AI factories.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e68 [third_party_view; futurumgroup.com (analyst); original source futurumgroup.com (analyst) ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Sharon AI is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs in new Australian capacity, and Firmus is building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned campus in Batam targeting up to 170,000 GPUs; both are among the first partners in NVIDIA’s model.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia, and Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs, are among the first partners.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e33; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e69 [third_party_view; futurumgroup.com (analyst); original source futurumgroup.com (analyst) ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Under the model introduced July 1, NVIDIA earns its standard hardware margin plus a recurring share of cloud income generated by supported capacity.\\n    Quote: Under the July 1 revenue-sharing and credit-support model, NVIDIA earns its standard hardware margin plus a recurring, usage-linked share of the cloud income the supported capacity generates.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e41; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e70 [party_statement; futurumgroup.com (analyst); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said the cost of a 1-gigawatt AI factory has risen from $20–30 billion to $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt; he also expects 100 gigawatts to be online before the decade ends.\\n    Quote: Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade.\",\n   \"- e71 [third_party_view; futurumgroup.com (analyst); original source futurumgroup.com (analyst) ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Firmus has $25–30 billion in committed off-take, according to the analyst, which the analyst says may or may not convert into recognized revenue on schedule.\\n    Quote: Whether Firmus’s $25–30 billion in committed off-take converts into recognized revenue on schedule.\",\n   \"- e33 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI and Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] The AI Compute Partnership's first adopters include Sharon AI, which plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW Batam campus targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e27, e42, e68, e114; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e36 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Firmus Technologies is constructing a Batam campus to Nvidia DGX SuperPOD (DSX) specifications, with plans for up to 360MW of capacity and up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus Technologies, a private company, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to Nvidia's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference architecture specifications. The facility is expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [3] .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e24, e43, e115; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e113 [party_statement; Futuriom, quoting NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress and VP of Global AI Clouds and Infrastructure Ecosystem Raj Mirpuri; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress and VP of Global AI Clouds and Infrastructure Ecosystem Raj Mirpuri 2026-07-08; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress and VP of Global AI Clouds and Infrastructure Ecosystem Raj Mirpuri said the new AI-cloud model provides NVIDIA with standard product revenue and a share of cloud revenue on supported capacity.\\n    Quote: “This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e19; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e114 [fact; Futuriom; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Sharon AI plans to buy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs through NVIDIA's AI-cloud revenue-sharing and credit-support plan.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI, based in Australia, will buy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under the plan.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e33; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e115 [fact; Futuriom; original source Firmus ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Firmus is building a DSX AI factory in Indonesia expected to scale to 360 megawatts and contain up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus, based in Indoesia, is building a DSX AI factory there that’s expected to scale to 360 megawatts and contain up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e36; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e118 [third_party_view; Futuriom; original source Futuriom 2026-07-08; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Futuriom judged that NVIDIA's new AI-cloud business model could increase GPU demand from startups and other small enterprises.\\n    Quote: Futuriom Take: NVIDIA’s new business model for AI cloud providers could boost demand for its GPUs among startups and other small enterprises.\",\n   \"- e13 [fact; qz.com; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies plans a campus in Batam, Indonesia, with capacity for up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs and 360 megawatts of power.\\n    Quote: A campus being developed by Firmus Technologies in Batam, Indonesia is planned at a capacity of up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs and 360 megawatts of power.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5, e17, e28, e359; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e61 [party_statement; SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Firmus expects $25–30 billion of customer revenue over six years from its project; SemiAnalysis expects Firmus to share some revenue above the NVIDIA backstop level with NVIDIA.\\n    Quote: Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e360; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e358 [party_statement; Jakarta Globe, quoting Airlangga Hartarto; original source Airlangga Hartarto, Indonesia Chief Economic Affairs Minister ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Indonesia Chief Economic Affairs Minister Airlangga Hartarto said the country has about 1.3 GW of planned data-center capacity, including the Batam project, representing $15 billion to $20 billion of investment; current operational capacity is about 580 MW.\\n    Quote: Indonesia currently has around 580 MW of operational data center capacity. The additional 1.3 GW that is in the pipeline, including the Batam project, represents investments of around $15 billion (Rp 271 trillion) to $20 billion\",\n   \"- e359 [fact; Jakarta Globe; original source Firmus Technologies and DayOne ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Firmus Technologies and DayOne are developing the Nvidia DSX AI Factory Campus, a planned 360 MW AI data-center complex in Batam.\\n    Quote: The Australian AI infrastructure company is partnering with Singapore-based data center operator DayOne to develop the Nvidia DSX AI Factory Campus, a 360-megawatt AI data center complex that is expected to become one of Southeast Asia's largest AI computing hubs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e13; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e360 [third_party_view; Jakarta Globe, relaying The Straits Times; original source The Straits Times ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] The Straits Times projected that the Batam campus could generate up to $30 billion in off-take agreements during its first six years of operation.\\n    Quote: According to The Straits Times , the project is projected to generate as much as $30 billion in off-take agreements during its first six years of operation.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e61; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e361 [party_statement; Jakarta Globe; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:e001119c6342373f6b1f3cf4 2026-07-14] Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation GPUs in phases from Q1 2027 through early 2028 for AI computing capacity in Indonesia.\\n    Quote: Under the partnership, Firmus plans to deploy about 170,000 of Nvidia's latest-generation graphics processing units (GPUs) in phases from the first quarter of 2027 through early 2028, providing AI computing capacity for startups and fast-growing AI companies.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e33; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 8,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a guarantee of OpenAI's Ohio data-center lease by 2027-06-30?\",\n   \"unit\": \"yes/no\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-06-30\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Yes\",\n     \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has signed the guarantee by the due day.\"\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"No\",\n     \"condition\": \"It has not.\"\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v2\",\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA sign a financial guarantee for OpenAI’s Ohio data-center lease by July 1, 2027?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"Whether NVIDIA signs a financial guarantee for OpenAI’s Ohio data-center lease\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2027-07-01\",\n   \"year\": \"2027\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"No\",\n    \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has not signed a financial guarantee for the Ohio data-center lease by 2027-07-01.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"Yes\",\n    \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has signed a financial guarantee for the Ohio data-center lease by 2027-07-01.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i005\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n    \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\",\n    \"passage\": \"NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA (per The Information)\",\n     \"what\": \"negotiating to guarantee OpenAI's lease of a large Ohio data center\",\n     \"how_much\": \"the data center could cost as much as $500 billion if fully built (asset cost, not the guarantee amount)\",\n     \"condition\": \"in negotiation; nothing signed\",\n     \"status\": \"rumour\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i006\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-01\",\n    \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\",\n    \"passage\": \"NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"what\": \"guarantees of customer data center leases, in exchange for rights to buy their shares\",\n     \"how_much\": \"an additional $3.5 billion\",\n     \"period\": \"quarter ended April 2026 (filed May)\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i116\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n     \"what\": \"NVIDIA backstops datacenter leases for neoclouds (a second kind of obligation)\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i119\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"SemiAnalysis\",\n     \"what\": \"more NVIDIA backstops exist but are not public; many neoclouds in negotiation; announcements accelerating\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\",\n     \"status_note\": \"existing undisclosed backstops; more deals are a forecast\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e6 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source The Information ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] The Information recently reported that NVIDIA was negotiating to guarantee OpenAI’s lease of a large Ohio data center; the facility could cost up to $500 billion if fully built out at current prices.\\n    Quote: According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\",\n   \"- e13 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Nvidia said on 2026-07-02 that it was introducing a partnership program using AI cloud providers to give AI startups access to computing infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Chipmaker Nvidia is expanding its support for fast-growing AI startups by introducing a partnership program that leverages its network of AI cloud service providers to offer these firms access to critical computing infrastructure, the company said Thursday.\",\n   \"- e14 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Under Nvidia’s new AI-cloud partnership program, participating firms will share product and cloud revenue with Nvidia; the article does not specify an amount or period.\\n    Quote: Cloud-based AI firms, model builders and other enterprises will share both product and cloud revenue with Nvidia, which is positioning itself as an intermediary helping startups gain direct access to full-stack computing powered by Nvidia chips.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1, e19, e25; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e15 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Sharon AI and Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] The two initial AI-cloud partners plan to provide compute for Nvidia’s program: Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs, and Firmus is building a Batam data center expected to reach 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs, while AI infrastructure company Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e20 [fact; Tom's Hardware author Luke James; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a revenue-sharing and credit-support model in which participating AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and NVIDIA receives a percentage of revenue generated by that infrastructure, in addition to hardware sales.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has announced a new business model under which it’ll be able to double-dip for revenue on the same silicon: once when its partner AI clouds use its hardware, and again as an ongoing percentage of the revenue that hardware generates. In a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress, the company pitched the “revenue-sharing and credit-support model” as a way to open compute access to startups \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e19; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e21 [fact; Tom's Hardware author Luke James; original source Sharon AI 2026-06-12; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI disclosed an agreement with NVIDIA running for six years and covering 72 MW of new Australian data-center capacity, with capacity to scale to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e9; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e22 [fact; Tom's Hardware author Luke James; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that’s expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e25 [party_statement; NVIDIA, in a post quoted by mitrade.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] NVIDIA said it is partnering with AI clouds on large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories using revenue-sharing and credit-support arrangements; the statement did not give an amount or period.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories through revenue-sharing and credit-support. This opens up compute access to the… pic.twitter.com/PSQdTQJTga\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e14; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e26 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs; no deployment date was given.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e27 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] Firmus is developing an Indonesia campus that could support approximately 170,000 GPUs and 360 MW of power capacity; no completion date was given.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is developing a major campus in Indonesia that could support approximately 170,000 GPUs and 360 megawatts of power capacity.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e28 [third_party_view; SixSigmaCapital, in a post quoted by mitrade.com; original source SixSigmaCapital 2026-07-02; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] SixSigmaCapital said NVIDIA’s model gives neoclouds more certainty but less upside, and reduces NVIDIA’s reliance on hyperscalers; it gave no figures.\\n    Quote: $NVDA Simplistically what this means for neoclouds,in my view, is it gives them certainty but takes away some of the upside. I think that’s a good deal for them.Also reduces $NVDA reliance on hyper scalers. So $META news yesterday almost irrelevant now too https://t.co/Xpz1WfCHhx\",\n   \"- e20 [fact; Tom's Hardware author Luke James; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a revenue-sharing and credit-support model in which participating AI clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and NVIDIA receives a percentage of revenue generated by that infrastructure, in addition to hardware sales.\\n    Quote: Nvidia has announced a new business model under which it’ll be able to double-dip for revenue on the same silicon: once when its partner AI clouds use its hardware, and again as an ongoing percentage of the revenue that hardware generates. In a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress, the company pitched the “revenue-sharing and credit-support model” as a way to open compute access to startups \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e19, e52; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e21 [fact; Tom's Hardware author Luke James; original source Sharon AI 2026-06-12; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI disclosed an agreement with NVIDIA running for six years and covering 72 MW of new Australian data-center capacity, with capacity to scale to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e9, e44; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e22 [fact; Tom's Hardware author Luke James; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that’s expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4, e54; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e78 [party_statement; SemiAnalysis; original source SharonAI ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SharonAI said its footprint is set to reach 132MW, including 102MW contracted, and it guided to more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs in total by mid-2027, including 40,000 backstopped GB300s.\\n    Quote: Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e15 (); both are in force: The two initial AI-cloud partners plan to provide compute for Nvidia’s program: Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs, and Firmus is building a Batam data center expected to reach 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\",\n   \"- e230 [party_statement; Futuriom, quoting NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress and VP of Global AI Clouds and Infrastructure Ecosystem Raj Mirpuri; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress and VP of Global AI Clouds and Infrastructure Ecosystem Raj Mirpuri ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress and VP Raj Mirpuri said the new AI-cloud partnership model combines credit support with revenue sharing, with NVIDIA earning standard product revenue plus a share of cloud revenue on supported capacity.\\n    Quote: “This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates \\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e19; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e231 [party_statement; Futuriom; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] NVIDIA said Sharon AI signed onto its new AI-cloud plan and will buy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA states that two AI cloud providers have already signed on for the new deal: Sharon AI, based in Australia, will buy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under the plan.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e44; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e232 [fact; Futuriom; original source Firmus ; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] Firmus is building a DSX AI factory in Indonesia expected to scale to 360 MW and contain up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Quote: Firmus, based in Indoesia, is building a DSX AI factory there that’s expected to scale to 360 megawatts and contain up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e54; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\"\n  ]\n },\n {\n  \"control\": 9,\n  \"ruler_question\": {\n   \"question\": \"Will Nvidia begin shipping its Rubin generation by December 31, 2026?\",\n   \"unit\": \"yes/no\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\",\n   \"rungs\": [\n    {\n     \"label\": \"Yes\",\n     \"condition\": \"Nvidia has begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    },\n    {\n     \"label\": \"No\",\n     \"condition\": \"Nvidia has not begun shipping Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n     \"lo\": null,\n     \"hi\": null\n    }\n   ]\n  },\n  \"judged_question\": {\n   \"view_id\": \"v3\",\n   \"question\": \"Will NVIDIA begin shipping its Vera Rubin platform by December 31, 2026?\",\n   \"looks_at\": \"Whether the Vera Rubin platform begins shipping by the end of 2026\",\n   \"due_day\": \"2026-12-31\",\n   \"year\": \"2026\",\n   \"kind\": \"asset\"\n  },\n  \"judged_rungs\": [\n   {\n    \"position\": 1,\n    \"label\": \"Yes\",\n    \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has begun shipping Vera Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   },\n   {\n    \"position\": 2,\n    \"label\": \"No\",\n    \"condition\": \"NVIDIA has not begun shipping Vera Rubin by December 31, 2026.\",\n    \"lo\": null,\n    \"hi\": null\n   }\n  ],\n  \"reader_b_decisive_items\": [\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i105\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-05\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA\",\n     \"what\": \"Vera Rubin systems in full production; shipping to eight cloud partners including AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud\",\n     \"period\": \"this fall (2026)\",\n     \"status\": \"happened (full production); plan (shipping this fall)\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i219\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Vera Rubin rack costs $7.8M — memory is why\",\n    \"passage\": \"First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"what\": \"Vera Rubin VR200 NVL72 first shipments and volume ramp\",\n     \"period\": \"first shipments Q3 2026; volume ramp Q4 2026\",\n     \"status\": \"plan\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i202\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\",\n    \"passage\": \"Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay.\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"Tom's Hardware\",\n     \"what\": \"scope of the reported delay: Rubin Ultra/Kyber only; 2026 Rubin on Oberon unaffected\",\n     \"period\": \"2026\",\n     \"status\": \"claim\"\n    }\n   },\n   {\n    \"id\": \"i207\",\n    \"day\": \"2026-07-06\",\n    \"title\": \"Nvidia Delays Next-Generation Kyber Server Architecture as Manufacturing Hurdles Give Rivals a Rare Opening\",\n    \"passage\": \"the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud\",\n    \"meaning\": {\n     \"who\": \"NVIDIA (as restated)\",\n     \"what\": \"Rubin systems in full production; first shipments to eight cloud partners incl. AWS, Azure, Google Cloud\",\n     \"period\": \"this fall\",\n     \"status\": \"happened\",\n     \"status_note\": \"in full production; shipping this fall is a plan\"\n    }\n   }\n  ],\n  \"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\": [\n   \"- e23 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-02; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] The author says NVIDIA’s Vera Rubin platform is in full production and could begin shipping by the end of 2026.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\",\n   \"- e45 [party_statement; Jonathan Sayers; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:a8fab49de53d125314a6688a 2026-07-04] NVIDIA announced that its DSX AI factories are hosting Rubin AI infrastructure in a design with no fans or cold aisles.\\n    Quote: it was announced that Nvidia DSX AI factories are hosting Nvidia's Rubin AI infrastructure with no fans or cold aisles.\",\n   \"- e61 [rumour; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA’s current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are due to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\",\n   \"- e64 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-05; from gnews:2f953a56cd06571ab0afc348 2026-07-05] The author says NVIDIA plans to launch its first stand-alone CPU as part of the Vera Rubin platform in fall 2026.\\n    Quote: In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform.\",\n   \"- e61 [rumour; CNBC; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:552f8c2bdfd080555d592587 2026-07-05] NVIDIA’s current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and are due to begin shipping in fall 2026 to eight cloud partners, including AWS, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\\n    Quote: Nvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e155; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e84 [third_party_view; MarketScale; original source MarketScale 2026-07-06; from gnews:f66e1689ec2e27e7eab2a2dd 2026-07-06] Vera Rubin rack first shipments are scheduled for Q3 2026, with volume ramping in Q4 2026.\\n    Quote: First shipments are scheduled for the third quarter of 2026, with volume ramping in the fourth.\",\n   \"- e107 [rumour; 247wallst.com (Danielle Liverance); original source Unnamed reports, as retold by 247wallst.com ; from gnews:3df0bbc8aff2c1d008bc0a32 2026-07-06] Reports cited by the author said NVIDIA’s upcoming Vera Rubin server systems were delayed; the item gives no timing or details.\\n    Quote: NVIDIA shares are under pressure on reports of delays across their upcoming lineup of Vera Rubin server systems.\",\n   \"- e155 [rumour; tikr.com; original source NVIDIA, as reported by tikr.com ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] NVIDIA’s current-generation Rubin systems were reported to be in full production and scheduled to start shipping in fall 2026 to eight major cloud partners, including AWS, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\\n    Quote: That’s likely because the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e61; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e191 [fact; fool.com (Jack Delaney); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:9026c944c63575a080ce7ba9 2026-07-07] Nvidia announced in March 2026 that it plans to launch its Vera Rubin Space-1 Module; no launch date was given.\\n    Quote: Also, Nvidia announced in March that it will be launching its Vera Rubin Space-1 Module at some point in the future.\",\n   \"- e155 [rumour; tikr.com; original source NVIDIA, as reported by tikr.com ; from gnews:d4c5dc540392f8df39aee250 2026-07-06] NVIDIA’s current-generation Rubin systems were reported to be in full production and scheduled to start shipping in fall 2026 to eight major cloud partners, including AWS, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\\n    Quote: That’s likely because the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e61, e224; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e224 [third_party_view; straitstimes.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:c8c39f971721fd820aab5c1c 2026-07-08] NVIDIA is reported to be working toward releasing its Rubin product line in the second half of 2026.\\n    Quote: Nvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 .\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e155; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e253 [third_party_view; moomoo.com; original source Bank of America ; from gnews:1b5a3359dd0644861c0fce42 2026-07-08] Bank of America estimated HBM content per rack would increase by about $200,000–$300,000 from Blackwell to Rubin, while total rack pricing would rise by $2 million–$3 million, from roughly $3 million–$4 million to $6 million–$7 million.\\n    Quote: The report estimates HBM content per rack could rise by about $0.2 million to $0.3 million from Blackwell to Rubin, while total rack pricing could increase by $2 million to $3 million, from roughly $3 million to $4 million for Blackwell to $6 million to $7 million for Vera Rubin.\",\n   \"- e389 [fact; barchart.com (author); original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:ededac685b09f7a9c6952202 2026-07-12] The author reported that NVIDIA had recently launched its Vera Rubin AI platform and released its RTX Spark AI PC platform, expanded partnerships with Alphabet and Marvell, and invested further in networking, CPUs, and AI infrastructure.\\n    Quote: Nvidia recently launched its Vera Rubin AI platform, released the AI PC platform RTX Spark, expanded partnerships with companies such as Alphabet (GOOGL) and Marvell (MRVL), and further invested in networking, CPUs, and AI infrastructure.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read it as a later version of e191 (); both are in force: Nvidia announced in March 2026 that it plans to launch its Vera Rubin Space-1 Module; no launch date was given.\",\n   \"- e390 [rumour; en.sedaily.com, citing the semiconductor industry; original source en.sedaily.com (unnamed-source report) 2026-07-12; from gnews:bf96def37a9c3a5f19f5d43c 2026-07-12] LG Electronics completed a Vera Rubin-specification AI server rack prototype in the first half of 2026 and plans to seek big-tech orders after second-half reliability evaluations, with mass production planned for 2027.\\n    Quote: According to the semiconductor industry on the 12th, LG Electronics' Production Research Institute (PRI) completed development of an AI server rack prototype matching the compute tray specifications of Nvidia's \\\"Vera Rubin\\\" in the first half of the year. LG plans to secure orders from global big tech companies after reliability evaluations in the second half and begin mass production next year.\",\n   \"- e23 [third_party_view; fool.com (analyst); original source fool.com (analyst) 2026-07-02; from gnews:af6e4910e877d5682a773d4b 2026-07-02] The author says NVIDIA’s Vera Rubin platform is in full production and could begin shipping by the end of 2026.\\n    Quote: Vera Rubin, Nvidia's next-generation AI chip platform, is in full production and could begin shipping later this year.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e501; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\",\n   \"- e473 [third_party_view; 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois); original source 247wallst.com (Alex Sirois) 2026-07-14; from gnews:ac8a489e54fbb1b85a1c7a2c 2026-07-14] The article said NVIDIA Blackwell 300 was ramping and Vera Rubin was queued to follow it.\\n    Quote: Blackwell 300 is ramping, and the Vera Rubin platform is queued behind it.\",\n   \"- e501 [third_party_view; EBC; original source EBC 2026-07-14; from gnews:f83ae0a4f01c18d48f20a629 2026-07-14] EBC reported that NVIDIA’s Vera Rubin architecture remained on track for shipment in late 2026.\\n    Quote: Blackwell Ultra and Rubin: Target shipping dates for Blackwell Ultra chips are ramping up fast and the Vera Rubin architecture remains officially on track for late 2026, but the market is currently priced for absolute perfection.\\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e23; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\"\n  ]\n }\n]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"controls/control-packet-4.json\\\"));\nfor x in p:\n print(\\\"CONTROL\\\",x[\\\"control\\\"]); print(\\\"RULER\\\",x[\\\"ruler_question\\\"][\\\"question\\\"],x[\\\"ruler_question\\\"][\\\"due_day\\\"]); print(\\\"JUDGED\\\",x[\\\"judged_question\\\"]); print(\\\"ITEMS\\\",[(i[\\\"id\\\"],i[\\\"passage\\\"],i[\\\"meaning\\\"]) for i in x[\\\"reader_b_decisive_items\\\"]]); print(\\\"EVIDENCE\\\"); print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(x[\\\"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\\\"]))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_1","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; p=json.load(open(\\\"controls/control-packet-4.json\\\"));\nfor x in p:\n print(\\\"CONTROL\\\",x[\\\"control\\\"]); print(\\\"RULER\\\",x[\\\"ruler_question\\\"][\\\"question\\\"],x[\\\"ruler_question\\\"][\\\"due_day\\\"]); print(\\\"JUDGED\\\",x[\\\"judged_question\\\"]); print(\\\"ITEMS\\\",[(i[\\\"id\\\"],i[\\\"passage\\\"],i[\\\"meaning\\\"]) for i in x[\\\"reader_b_decisive_items\\\"]]); print(\\\"EVIDENCE\\\"); print(\\\"\\\\n\\\".join(x[\\\"evidence_shown_to_the_judge_through_2026_07_14\\\"]))'\"","aggregated_output":"CONTROL 7\nRULER What total amount of financing commitments and capacity backstops to customers and partners will NVIDIA report outstanding at its FY2027 year-end? 2027-02-28\nJUDGED {'view_id': 'v2', 'question': \"At NVIDIA's FY2027 annual report, what amount will NVIDIA report for its outstanding customer data-center lease guarantees and related capacity-purchase commitments?\", 'looks_at': \"NVIDIA's credit-support and capacity-purchase commitments to customers, including the revenue-sharing and credit-support program\", 'due_day': '2027-02-28', 'year': 'FY2027', 'kind': 'asset'}\nITEMS [('i120', 'Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B', {'who': 'Sharon AI disclosed', 'what': \"total value of NVIDIA's backstop on Sharon's 72MW Australian AI factory (up to 40,000 GB300)\", 'how_much': '$4.88B; implied floor ~$2.33/hr/GPU', 'period': 'six years', 'condition': 'up to 40,000 GB300s', 'status': 'happened', 'status_note': 'announced June 2026'}), ('i122', 'Firmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop.', {'who': 'Firmus', 'what': 'a second announced NVIDIA-backstopped cluster', 'how_much': '360MW', 'period': 'announced June 29, 2026', 'status': 'happened', 'status_note': 'announced'}), ('i123', 'Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years', {'who': 'Firmus', 'what': 'expected customer revenue on the backstopped Batam cluster (part above the backstop is shared with NVIDIA)', 'how_much': '$25B-$30B', 'period': 'over six years', 'status': 'forecast'}), ('i119', 'Nvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia', {'who': 'SemiAnalysis', 'what': 'more NVIDIA backstops exist but are not public; many neoclouds in negotiation; announcements accelerating', 'status': 'claim', 'status_note': 'existing undisclosed backstops; more deals are a forecast'}), ('i005', 'NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio', {'who': 'NVIDIA (per The Information)', 'what': \"negotiating to guarantee OpenAI's lease of a large Ohio data center\", 'how_much': 'the data center could cost as much as $500 billion if fully built (asset cost, not the guarantee amount)', 'condition': 'in negotiation; nothing signed', 'status': 'rumour'})]\nEVIDENCE\n- e2 [fact; moomoo.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] NVIDIA announced a business model combining revenue sharing with credit support for AI cloud providers.\n    Quote: On July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\n- e3 [rumour; moomoo.com; original source The Information 2026-07-01; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Firmus and Sharon AI joined NVIDIA's revenue-sharing and credit-support program, according to the report, with three executives with existing NVIDIA business ties confirming the arrangement.\n    Quote: GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\n- e4 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Quote: Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs\n- e5 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, planned to reach up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n    Quote: Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n- e7 [fact; moomoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] NVIDIA's filing submitted in May 2026, covering the quarter ended in April, reported that it added $3.5 billion in guarantees for customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\n    Quote: According to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\n- e4 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Quote: Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e12; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e5 [party_statement; moomoo.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:4d1677af37befd53ca3e7b45 2026-07-01] Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, planned to reach up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n    Quote: Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e13; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e8 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a revenue-sharing program intended to give AI startups, model builders, and enterprises access to its computing infrastructure without financing hardware buildouts.\n    Quote: Nvidia $NVDA announced a revenue-sharing program on Wednesday designed to give AI startups, model builders, and enterprises access to its computing infrastructure without requiring them to finance hardware buildouts themselves.\n- e9 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] Under NVIDIA's revenue-sharing arrangement, NVIDIA receives revenue from product sales and cloud operations provided by AI-cloud partners to end customers.\n    Quote: Revenue from both product sales and cloud operations flows back to Nvidia under the arrangement, as AI cloud companies provide Nvidia -powered services to their end customers.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e16, e19; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e10 [party_statement; qz.com; original source Colette Kress ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress described the program's income as a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream and said it lets customers access large-scale compute without handling site selection, power procurement, construction, and hardware bring-up.\n    Quote: Kress, writing in a blog post, called the resulting income a \"recurring, usage-linked earnings stream\" for Nvidia ; on the customer side, she said the model removes the need to navigate site selection, power procurement, construction, and hardware bring-up before accessing large-scale compute.\n- e11 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA's offering includes token credits that let startups access compute resources without upfront capital outlays.\n    Quote: Token credits are also part of the offering, giving startups a way to access compute resources without upfront capital outlays.\n- e12 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA; Sharon AI ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA named Sharon AI as an initial partner, and Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Quote: Nvidia named two initial partners. Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e4, e21, e27; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e13 [fact; qz.com; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] Firmus Technologies plans a campus in Batam, Indonesia, with capacity for up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs and 360 megawatts of power.\n    Quote: A campus being developed by Firmus Technologies in Batam, Indonesia is planned at a capacity of up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs and 360 megawatts of power.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e5, e17, e28; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e14 [party_statement; qz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA said AI demand is shifting from model development toward production inference workloads that require continuously operating infrastructure at scale.\n    Quote: Nvidia framed the program as a response to a structural shift in AI demand, from model development toward production inference workloads that require continuously operating infrastructure at scale.\n- e15 [fact; CNBC; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] NVIDIA said it was introducing a partnership program to connect AI startups with computing infrastructure from AI cloud service providers.\n    Quote: Chipmaker Nvidia is expanding its support for fast-growing AI startups by introducing a partnership program that leverages its network of AI cloud service providers to offer these firms access to critical computing infrastructure, the company said Thursday.\n- e16 [party_statement; CNBC; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Under the announced program, cloud-based AI firms, model builders and other enterprises will share product and cloud revenue with NVIDIA.\n    Quote: Cloud-based AI firms, model builders and other enterprises will share both product and cloud revenue with Nvidia, which is positioning itself as an intermediary helping startups gain direct access to full-stack computing powered by Nvidia chips.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e9, e22; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e17 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Sharon AI and Firmus Technologies 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA GPUs; Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam expected to reach 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n    Quote: Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs, while AI infrastructure company Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e13, e20, e24; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e22 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a revenue-sharing and credit-support model intended to help startups access compute.\n    Quote: In a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress, the company pitched the “revenue-sharing and credit-support model” as a way to open compute access to startups that can’t finance it themselves.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e16; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e23 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Sharon AI 2026-06-12; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI disclosed a six-year agreement covering 72 MW of new Australian data-center capacity, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Quote: In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e24 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that’s expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e17; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e26 [fact; mitrade.com; original source NVIDIA 2026-07-02; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced an AI compute framework using revenue-sharing and credit-support agreements to help cloud providers deploy advanced hardware.\n    Quote: NVIDIA’s new AI compute framework allows cloud providers to deploy advanced hardware using revenue-sharing and credit-support agreements.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e19; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e27 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Quote: Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e12; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e28 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] Firmus is developing an Indonesia campus that could support approximately 170,000 GPUs and 360 megawatts of power capacity.\n    Quote: Firmus is developing a major campus in Indonesia that could support approximately 170,000 GPUs and 360 megawatts of power capacity.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e13; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e29 [third_party_view; SixSigmaCapital; original source SixSigmaCapital 2026-07-02; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] SixSigmaCapital said NVIDIA’s framework gives neoclouds greater certainty but less upside, and reduces NVIDIA’s reliance on hyperscalers.\n    Quote: $NVDA Simplistically what this means for neoclouds,in my view, is it gives them certainty but takes away some of the upside. I think that’s a good deal for them.Also reduces $NVDA reliance on hyper scalers.\n- e23 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Sharon AI 2026-06-12; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Sharon AI disclosed a six-year agreement covering 72 MW of new Australian data-center capacity, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Quote: In an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e21, e34; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e24 [fact; Tom's Hardware; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:3854aeddaa3f31de00cf2816 2026-07-02] Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n    Quote: Meanwhile, Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that’s expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e17, e36; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e27 [fact; mitrade.com; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:bf158d067256a39f02f7c7ce 2026-07-02] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Quote: Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e12, e33; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e30 [party_statement; mlq.ai; original source Nvidia 2026-07-01; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Nvidia announced the AI Compute Partnership on July 1, 2026, to backstop neocloud customers' GPU deployments in return for a recurring share of cloud revenue generated by those GPUs.\n    Quote: Nvidia on July 1 unveiled a new financing vehicle it calls the \"AI Compute Partnership,\" under which the chipmaker acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments and, in return, takes a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate [1] .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e31 [party_statement; mlq.ai; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Under Nvidia's AI Compute Partnership, Nvidia agrees to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate, backstopping lenders' exposure to neocloud deployments.\n    Quote: The backstop mechanism works by Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate, effectively de-risking lender exposure and making it easier for smaller cloud operators to secure capital for large GPU deployments [1] [3] .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e18; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e32 [party_statement; mlq.ai; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Nvidia did not disclose the revenue-share percentage for the AI Compute Partnership and declined to provide details beyond its blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress.\n    Quote: The revenue-share percentage was not disclosed; Nvidia declined to provide details beyond its blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress [3]\n- e33 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI and Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] The AI Compute Partnership's first adopters include Sharon AI, which plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW Batam campus targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Quote: Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e27; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e34 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under a six-year agreement with Nvidia.\n    Quote: Sharon AI, which listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under a six-year agreement with Nvidia [2] [6] .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e23; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e35 [fact; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Sharon AI raised $1.6 billion in a June 2026 private placement to fund its Nvidia-based AI factory.\n    Quote: The company raised an additional $1.6 billion in private placement in June 2026 to fund its Nvidia-based AI factory\n- e36 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Firmus Technologies is constructing a Batam campus to Nvidia DGX SuperPOD (DSX) specifications, with plans for up to 360MW of capacity and up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Quote: Firmus Technologies, a private company, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to Nvidia's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference architecture specifications. The facility is expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [3] .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e24; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e33 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI and Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] The AI Compute Partnership's first adopters include Sharon AI, which plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW Batam campus targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Quote: Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e27, e42; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e36 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Firmus Technologies is constructing a Batam campus to Nvidia DGX SuperPOD (DSX) specifications, with plans for