e argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e43 [fact; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Firmus ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Firmus is developing a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to reach 360 MW and accommodate up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs; no completion date is given.\n    Quote: Firmus is developing a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e36; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e8 [fact; qz.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:dde08c03c8dd6f66031632d5 2026-07-02] NVIDIA announced a revenue-sharing program intended to give AI startups, model builders, and enterprises access to its computing infrastructure without financing hardware buildouts.\n    Quote: Nvidia $NVDA announced a revenue-sharing program on Wednesday designed to give AI startups, model builders, and enterprises access to its computing infrastructure without requiring them to finance hardware buildouts themselves.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e50; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e17 [party_statement; CNBC; original source Sharon AI and Firmus Technologies 2026-07-02; from gnews:d1364ee37c17c361ae74f563 2026-07-02] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA GPUs; Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam expected to reach 360 MW and house up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\n    Quote: Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia GPUs, while AI infrastructure company Firmus Technologies says it is building a data center in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e13, e20, e24, e60; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e33 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI and Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] The AI Compute Partnership's first adopters include Sharon AI, which plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW Batam campus targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Quote: Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e27, e42, e68; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e34 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under a six-year agreement with Nvidia.\n    Quote: Sharon AI, which listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under a six-year agreement with Nvidia [2] [6] .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e23, e59; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e40 [fact; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Nvidia launched a revenue-sharing financing model for AI cloud providers; the report gives no amount or date for the launch.\n    Quote: Nvidia has launched a revenue-sharing financing model aimed at AI cloud providers, facilitating access to its computing infrastructure.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e19, e52; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e41 [fact; datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com; original source Nvidia ; from gnews:1e7a9c953f3ad70093b67ae4 2026-07-04] Nvidia's financing model lets AI cloud operators procure its infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to cloud services sold using that capacity.\n    Quote: Under this financing structure, AI cloud operators can procure Nvidia infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to the cloud services sold based on that capacity.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e19, e69; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e50 [fact; finance.yahoo.com; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:68a3ed21f77b935d9fa7ec5e 2026-07-06] NVIDIA had launched a revenue-sharing compute program for AI startups; the item gives no launch date or program amount.\n    Quote: The chipmaker, fresh off launching a revenue-sharing compute program for AI startups, has faced this script before.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e8; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e52 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started providing neoclouds take-or-pay minimum-revenue guarantees on GPU rental capacity in exchange for a share of revenue above the backstop level.\n    Quote: This is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e40; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e53 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source NVIDIA ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis reports that NVIDIA has started backstopping neocloud data-center leases.\n    Quote: Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\n- e54 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis says NVIDIA’s backstop program typically lasts six years and commits NVIDIA to purchase compute at pre-agreed prices that vary over the term.\n    Quote: Nvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period.\n- e55 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis says lenders underwrite NVIDIA-backstopped GPU financing to a minimum 1.3x debt-service coverage ratio, typically corresponding to a 70–80% loan-to-value ratio.\n    Quote: When it comes to the Nvidia backstop, banks look at the DSCR ratios assuming revenue earned under the scenario where the backstop is activated. They are looking for a DSCR of at least 1.3x, particularly for the first few years of GPU financing agreement and size their loans to ensure that this bar is met. This typically corresponds to a 70-80% loan to value (LTV) ratio.\n- e56 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis models a 25.4% project IRR for a neocloud’s one-year-or-shorter GPU rental book with NVIDIA’s backstop, potentially higher if rental rates decline less than modeled.\n    Quote: We see that the short-term 1y rental book scenario clearly offers the best IRR among the backstopped scenarios at 25.4%, and these IRRs could realize even higher if the rental market turns out to be strong enough such that 1y and shorter rental rates decay by less than we modeled in the curve below.\n- e57 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis models up to a 40.7% IRR for one-year GPU rentals without a backstop, versus zero or negative IRRs if a neocloud must rent to NVIDIA under the backstop.\n    Quote: As expected, the IRRs are higher without a backstop – reaching as high as 40.7% when renting for 1y tenors with no backstop. But if Neoclouds activate the backstop and rent to Nvidia at those prices, they can expect to earn zero or negative IRRs.\n- e58 [third_party_view; SemiAnalysis; original source SemiAnalysis 2026-07-06; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SemiAnalysis models neocloud profit-before-tax margins falling from 14.8% to 5.4% when financing cost rises from 5.62% to 10%.\n    Quote: In our modelling, we show that increasing funding from an all-in cost of 5.62% to an all-in cost of 10% drops PBT margins from 14.8% down to only 5.4%.\n- e59 [party_statement; SemiAnalysis; original source SharonAI ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] SharonAI’s June 2026 Australia AI-factory deal includes a six-year NVIDIA backstop for up to 40,000 GB300s valued at $4.88 billion, or about $2.33 per GPU-hour on average; SharonAI says its footprint is set to reach 132 MW, with 102 MW contracted, and guides to more than 55,000 NVIDIA GPUs by mid-2027.\n    Quote: The first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e34; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e60 [fact; SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus 2026-06-29; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Firmus announced a 360 MW AI cluster project in Batam, Indonesia, on June 29, 2026, as part of the NVIDIA-backstop rollout.\n    Quote: Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e17; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e61 [party_statement; SemiAnalysis; original source Firmus ; from gnews:085b1e1a1543437edb9756d6 2026-07-06] Firmus expects $25–30 billion of customer revenue over six years from its project; SemiAnalysis expects Firmus to share some revenue above the NVIDIA backstop level with NVIDIA.\n    Quote: Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\n- e67 [fact; futurumgroup.com (analyst); original source NVIDIA 2026-07-01; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA introduced a revenue-sharing and credit-support model for AI-cloud operators deploying AI factories on July 1, 2026.\n    Quote: On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA introduced a business model that lets AI cloud operators deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories through a combination of revenue sharing and credit support, with the resulting facilities built as DSX AI factories.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e1; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e68 [third_party_view; futurumgroup.com (analyst); original source futurumgroup.com (analyst) ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Sharon AI is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs in new Australian capacity, and Firmus is building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned campus in Batam targeting up to 170,000 GPUs; both are among the first partners in NVIDIA’s model.\n    Quote: Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia, and Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs, are among the first partners.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e33; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e69 [third_party_view; futurumgroup.com (analyst); original source futurumgroup.com (analyst) ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Under the model introduced July 1, NVIDIA earns its standard hardware margin plus a recurring share of cloud income generated by supported capacity.\n    Quote: Under the July 1 revenue-sharing and credit-support model, NVIDIA earns its standard hardware margin plus a recurring, usage-linked share of the cloud income the supported capacity generates.\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e41; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e70 [party_statement; futurumgroup.com (analyst); original source Jensen Huang ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] NVIDIA CEO Jensen Huang said the cost of a 1-gigawatt AI factory has risen from $20–30 billion to $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt; he also expects 100 gigawatts to be online before the decade ends.\n    Quote: Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade.\n- e71 [third_party_view; futurumgroup.com (analyst); original source futurumgroup.com (analyst) ; from gnews:6ab3379f10dfeb28924ebb57 2026-07-06] Firmus has $25–30 billion in committed off-take, according to the analyst, which the analyst says may or may not convert into recognized revenue on schedule.\n    Quote: Whether Firmus’s $25–30 billion in committed off-take converts into recognized revenue on schedule.\n- e33 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Sharon AI and Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] The AI Compute Partnership's first adopters include Sharon AI, which plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW Batam campus targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Quote: Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e27, e42, e68, e114; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e36 [third_party_view; mlq.ai; original source Firmus Technologies ; from gnews:9e0d5b0d6b3e603c60340128 2026-07-03] Firmus Technologies is constructing a Batam campus to Nvidia DGX SuperPOD (DSX) specifications, with plans for up to 360MW of capacity and up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\n    Quote: Firmus Technologies, a private company, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to Nvidia's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference architecture specifications. The facility is expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [3] .\n    Opinion, not verified: the matcher read this as the same claim as e24, e43, e115; the argument counts one claim, one more outlet.\n- e113 [party_statement; Futuriom, quoting NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress and VP of Global AI Clouds and Infrastructure Ecosystem Raj Mirpuri; original source NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress and VP of Global AI Clouds and Infrastructure Ecosystem Raj Mirpuri 2026-07-08; from gnews:cb05b9f49943ef0ba412cf36 2026-07-08] NVIDIA CFO Colette Kress and VP of Global AI Clouds and Infrastructure Ecosystem Raj Mirpuri said the new AI-cloud model provides NVIDIA with standard product revenue and a share of cloud revenue on supported capacity.\n    Quote: “This new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product 